.TH LIKEOS-INSTALL.CONF 5 "November 2026" "LikeOS" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
likeos-install.conf \- answers for an unattended LikeOS installation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B likeos-install \-\-answers
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
An answer file holds everything
.BR likeos-install (1)
would otherwise ask: the disk to install on, the language, the time zone,
the keyboard, the computer's name, the root password and the account to
sign in with.
Given with
.BR \-\-answers ,
it makes the installation unattended.
.PP
The file is UTF-8 text with one setting per line,
.IP
.IB key = value
.PP
Blanks around the key and the value are ignored.
Empty lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
A value may be put in double quotes; inside them
.B \e"
stands for a quote,
.B \e\e
for a backslash and
.B \en
for a new line, and blanks and
.B #
are kept.
After the closing quote only blanks and a comment may follow.
An unknown key, a bad value or a line without
.B =
stops the installation before anything is changed, with the file name and
line number.
.PP
Every key may be left out; the default is given below.
.SH KEYS
.SS The disk
.TP
.BR disk= \fIdevice\fR | auto
The disk to install on:
.BR /dev/sda ,
.B sda
or
.BR /dev/nvme0n1 .
.B auto
(the default) takes the only disk LikeOS can be installed on, and is
refused when there is more than one.
The disk the installer was started from cannot be chosen.
.TP
.BR whole_disk= yes | no
.B yes
(the default) erases the whole disk: a new GUID partition table, an EFI
system partition of 512 MiB (FAT32) and the LikeOS root file system (ext4)
in the rest.
.B no
installs on one partition of the disk, given with
.BR root_partition ;
the other partitions are kept.
.TP
.BI root_partition= n
With
.BR whole_disk=no :
the number of the partition LikeOS goes to.
It is formatted with ext4 (everything on it is lost) and must be large
enough for the system.
The disk must have an EFI system partition (FAT, at least 100 MB) apart
from it; the boot loader is put there next to what it holds already.
.TP
.BR reboot= yes | no
Restart the computer into the installed system when done (default
.BR yes ).
.SS Language and region
.TP
.BI locale= locale
The language of the installed system, as a locale name (default
.BR en_US.UTF\-8 ).
.TP
.BI time_format= locale
How dates, times and numbers are shown (default
.BR en_US.UTF\-8 ).
.TP
.BI timezone= zone
The time zone, as a name of the time zone database:
.BR Europe/Berlin ,
.BR America/New_York ,
.B UTC
(the default).
.SS Keyboard
.TP
.BI keyboard= layout\fR[\fB,\fIlayout\fR...]
The keyboard layouts, up to four, separated by commas, as XKB layout names:
.BR us ,
.BR de ,
.BR fr ,
.B gb
\&... (default
.BR us ).
With more than one, Alt+Shift switches between them.
.TP
.BI keyboard_variant= variant\fR[\fB,\fIvariant\fR...]
The variant of each layout, in the same order, an empty one for the
layout's own:
.B nodeadkeys,
for
.BR keyboard=de,us .
When given, there must be as many as there are layouts.
.TP
.BI keyboard_options= options
XKB options, separated by commas
.RB ( grp:alt_shift_toggle ,
.BR caps:escape ...).
.SS This computer
.TP
.BI hostname= name
The computer's name: letters, digits and hyphens, at most 63, with at
least one letter, not starting or ending with a hyphen, no dots (no domain)
and not
.BR localhost .
The default is
.B likeos\-
followed by four random hexadecimal digits.
.TP
.BI root_password= password
The password of root, as plain text.
.TP
.BI root_password_hash= hash
The password of root as a
.BR crypt (3)
hash
.RB ( $y$ ...
yescrypt,
.B $6$
\&...), used when
.B root_password
is not given.
One of the two is required.
.SS The account
The account to sign in with.
It is created when
.B user_name
(or
.BR user_fullname )
is given, and then needs a password too.
.TP
.BI user_fullname= name
The full name of the person
.RB ( "\(dqErika Mustermann\(dq" ).
.TP
.BI user_name= name
The user name: lower-case letters, digits, hyphens and underscores,
starting with a letter or an underscore, at most 32, and not the name of a
system account.
.TP
.BI user_password= password
The account's password, as plain text.
.TP
.BI user_password_hash= hash
The account's password as a
.BR crypt (3)
hash, used when
.B user_password
is not given.
.TP
.BR user_extra_groups= yes | no
Put the account in the groups
.BR audio ,
.B render
and
.B users
(default
.BR yes ),
so it can play sound and use the graphics hardware.
.SH SECURITY
An answer file with plain-text passwords gives them to anyone who can read
it.
Prefer the
.B _hash
keys; files the installer saves itself have mode 0600 and hold hashes
unless asked to keep the plain passwords.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
# likeos\-install.conf \-\- answers for an unattended LikeOS installation
# (likeos\-install \-\-answers FILE).  See likeos\-install.conf(5).

disk=/dev/nvme0n1
whole_disk=yes
reboot=yes

# Language, formats, time zone
locale=de_DE.UTF\-8
time_format=de_DE.UTF\-8
timezone=Europe/Berlin

# Keyboard
keyboard=de,us
keyboard_variant=nodeadkeys,
keyboard_options=grp:alt_shift_toggle

# This computer
hostname=office\-pc
root_password_hash=$y$j9T$...

# Your account
user_fullname="Erika Mustermann"
user_name=erika
user_password_hash=$y$j9T$...
user_extra_groups=yes
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR likeos-install (1),
.BR likeos-installer (1),
.BR crypt (3),
.BR fstab (5)
